Finally!

I started playing sporkhack once it was put on the NAO server, and it
just kept on beating me.  But by the time I started feeling
comfortable enough to pull off an ascension, I thought I could shoot
for some kind of extra conduct with a monk.

Weaponless didn't work out, I just get too eager with my pick-axe.  So
instead, it's vegetarian, no polypiling, and no artifact wishing.
(that one I usually do anyway...)

There might be a couple spoilers in here, of course.

I'd never played monks too often before, but I fell in love with them
in spork because of the gauntlets of force.  I was flinging monsters
out of my way and all over the map - or just barely out of melee range
with some bigger monsters.  This was VERY handy whenever I ran into
the hard-hitting boojums.  They used to beat the crap out of my
characters before. But now I'd just bump them a square away, wait for
them to step back in range, smack them away again, rinse and repeat.
(this was good for mind flayers and other nasties too)  It could get a
bit annoying with phasing monsters, but sending them flying through a
wall was too fun for me to think of taking the gloves off.

I found three magic markers in this game, which I used to genocide the
usual L class (takes 2 scrolls to finish the job in spork though) and
pick off a couple special annoyances that have new cursing and armor-
spoiling powers in spork.  I also genocided foo-trices out of spite
because my last monk was taken out by a troll with a rubber chicken
while searching all over for the vibrating square.  Unfortunately, I
wasn't allowed to genocide the magical eyes, which were a particular
pain for me when I had the Eyes of the Overworld on.

I did a lot of wandering and backtracking in this game, just playing
it slow and cautious.  I kept a stash of future polyfodder in Sokoban
but decided later to not bother with it.  I had a nice stash of wishes
now to fill in whatever gaps I had in my kit.  I ignored the quest for
a long time - I often have bad luck with the quest in general, and
Master Kaen in particular.  Meanwhile, I'd got my charisma up high
enough to start sleeping my way to the top.  The foocubi seem trickier
in spork, but I can't say for sure.  The first one I found canceled
out very quickly, but the next two kept going for ages. I ended up
having two of them wandering around the "big room" level (one got
scared down a trap door from the level above), which was an excellent
setup.  I could always see where they'd run off to, and while one was
complaining about a headache I'd go chase down the other.  I got all
the way to level 30 from these two and still neither of them had
canceled!  So I just left them there and went back to do the quest at
last.

Once I took out Kaen, I camped out at the altar on the home level for
a while.  I hadn't done much sacrificing because the enchanted
"ordinary" gifts from the gods were often useless for my monks.  But
now that I was at level 30 I was sure to get an artifact... right?  I
wanted Stormbringer to protect myself up against the new buffed-up
Vlad's level drain, which had been a pain for me before.  But the
first artifact I got was... Dragonbane.  Whoop de doo.  I wasn't
having any luck getting any more gifts.  But then I realized I already
had a spellbook of restore ability.  I could get Stormbringer from
being crowned, so I started just praying whenever I got my timer back
until it happened.

Gehennom took a long time, but the new levels with the lava made
things interesting.  The Nazgul were particularly annoying here
though, with their new stunning spam attack. I managed to kill all
nine of them before starting the endgame, compared to vanilla where I
usually only find 2-4 of them.  It's nice to see all of them in
action, especially when their arrival is a lot more dramatic now. It
made for one YAFM too - I was just opening a tin to see if it was
spinach when a new Nazgul showed up.  Somehow I forgot what the exact
wording is for their attack, but it was something like this:

"You start opening the tin. You hear a terrific scream!"

I put off Vlad's tower for a while too, in favor of the standard main-
branch dungeon crawl and looking for the vibrating square.  It took me
an incredibly long time again for some reason.  In the meantime, my
worst enemy was the nalfeshnee's.  Their summoning nasties is annoying
enough, but when I'd get close to bitchslap some sense into them,
they'd ruin my beautiful fireproof speed boots!  I started using
spells or wands to take them out from a distance instead.  Finally I
found the square, marked a path to it with some iron chains, and tried
to remember where Vlad's tower was again.  To take him out I wielded
Stormbringer just for defense, then zapped him with wands.

So now it was time for the wizard.  One upside to all my wandering and
putzing around was I'd built up a nice stash of wands of death. (and
teleportation, for later)  So things were pretty straightforward from
here on out.  Got the book, got the amulet, and ran for the upstairs.
(good riddance, mysterious force!)  Earth and Fire were easy, and the
air elementals weren't that much trouble for me - they only seemed to
get in one really strong volley of hits on me each.  Maybe because of
the monk's stunning attack?  In the water plane I got boxed in pretty
heavily by the water elementals, but made it through okay.

It wasn't until I got to the Astral Plane that I was REALLY worried.
I took a minute to go through my inventory again and dumped a bunch of
things I knew I didn't need any more-  that way I could afford to take
some more useful items out of my bag of holding.  I still had leftover
wishes, so I wished for a ring of increase damage and charged it up to
a healthy +6. I had one amulet of life saving that I hadn't been using
- staying away from armor and shields by getting reflection from my
amulet usually works a lot better at saving my monk's life.  But now
that it was down to the wire and I was paranoid and frightened, I
figured I better have some backup.  I went with a shield for
reflection so I could still go armor-less.  I couldn't smack everybody
out of my way anymore, but this is what I'd been saving up all those
wands of teleportation for.  I faced Famine first, who went down fast,
but it wasn't the right altar. So I went for Pestilence's turf next.
He had me worried since casting cure sickness wasn't happening with my
shield on.  But I took him out fast and cured myself with a unicorn
horn.  I figured this must be the right altar when I tried zapping a
priest out of the door and made the other priest behind him angry, so
I ran around him and sure enough - chaotic altar!  Yatta!


Mijae, chaotic female human Monk




